In article <3744@aplcomm.jhuapl.edu>, jcn@kossy.jhuapl.edu (Sunday's Bane) writes:
>In article <1115@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu> arrom@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu (Ken Arromdee) writes:
>>How do you keep players from colluding [...]
>
>I would add an ownership attribute to each of the objects in the game.  This
>attribute could attach an object to a particular player in the game; no other
>player would be allowed to pick up that object.  Some objects in the dungeon
>would allow anyone to hold them; this would allow some amount of sharing
>without allowing one character to get too strong too fast.

An alternative would be for each player to `own' what s/he comes in with and
anything which is picked up. Anything lying around on a level is unowned until
it is picked up. If a monster runs off with one of your possessions, it reverts
to unowned. Maybe there should be options for characters to steal from you
(thieves are very good at this, samurai are not so good) with some probability
of success, failure or discovery. Selling things in shops would also disown
them. People playing as a team should do better than people playing as
individuals in a group, by letting players pick up a particular item on their
suitability for using it (rather than one player grabbing everything in sight).

In article <2952@sun.soe.clarkson.edu>, spam@sun.soe!clutx.clarkson.edu (Roger Gonzalez,,,) writes:
>This is exactly the same thing I was thinking about.  This could be very 
>similar to the game "hunt" in architecture.  Unfortunartely, this changes
>the game into a "real-time" action game.  This is Not a Good Thing, especially
>when you are trying to figure out the optimal place to defend yourself against
>a treasure zoo.  I can't think of any other way to deal with monsters moving;
>you obviously can't have them move only when *you* do, and if they move when
>*anybody* moves, you'd be in trouble.  One suggestion was the "turn" approach.
>In this, the monsters would move after every person had done something.  
>I don't know how *you* play hack, but *I* take breaks every now and then.
>Twould be a pity to come back from the fridge and find yourself dead.
>
This is the major problem with multiplayer nethack; not implementation (like
shopkeeper bills, etc.) but how the game is played. I take breaks whilst
playing (making coffee, answering peoples' questions, etc.) but I can also see
myself getting extremely irritated waiting for someone else to make a cuppa
whilst I want to move. The only way round this would be for everyone to agree
what time to play, how long a session will be, etc. I would love to play a
multi-user version of nethack, but it must be recognised that the way it is
played will have to be different. It's no longer a case of playing when you have
a free half hour and then saving the game for later, the time to start must be
pre-arranged and everyone must agree when to save. I would willingly accept
these restrictions (as long as I can play single player games as well) and
people tolerate them in other games (D&D, Diplomacy, etc.), so why not?

